1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
In this lesson, you will build a RAG system, or Retrieval Augmented Generation System using Pinecone.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
You will work with a sample dataset of Wikipedia articles and build vector embeddings out of the articles.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Then you will look at what search results look like by doing a simple document retrieval from Pinecone.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Then while working with OpenAI, you will build a nicely summarized article out of these results.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
All right, let's dive into the code.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Now we're going to build a classic Retrieval Augmented Generation, or RAG system, using Pinecone with OpenAI.

7
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.000
And it's going to look something like this.

8
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
So we have our happy-faced user that we had in previous lessons here on the left.

9
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
And what he's going to do is ask a question against a dataset that we will have prepared and stored in Pinecone.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
And in this case, we'll ask, what was the Berlin Wall?

11
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
And similar to other lessons, we got back long responses or documents from Pinecone.

12
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
But it would be really nice if we could take some of those long responses and get a summarized version of them.

13
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000
And what we'll do is show how to build and do a little bit of prompt engineering out of the responses from Pinecone,

14
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
send those back into the prompt of OpenAI, and get back a beautifully written response from OpenAI.

15
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.000
And this is really just RAG in a nutshell.

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
And I definitely encourage you to try different versions of doing prompt engineering.

17
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Ours is just a fairly simple example to show you what it might look like.

18
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
So let's get started.

19
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
So what we're going to do is first clear out warnings.

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.000
So we've seen this previously before where we import warnings and filter out the warnings.

21
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.000
And this is just to keep things clean for us.

22
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
So then we're going to pull in the packages that we rely on.

23
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
And, again, we have our deep learning AI tools package that manages keys from OpenAI and Pinecone for us.

24
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
And then we're going to set up Pinecone similar to how we have before.

25
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
So we're going to get the API key as so.

26
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Okay.

27
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Now that we have our Pinecone API key, we're going to, as usual, connect to Pinecone.

28
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
So first connect to Pinecone.

29
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Then get the index name using our utils package.

30
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:14.000
And then delete our index if it already exists.

31
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.000
And then create the index.

32
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.000
And then finally get a pointer to the index that we just created.

33
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Let's look at our index name just for fun.

34
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.000
All right.

35
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.000
Something long and unintelligible, but that's okay.

36
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
It works great nonetheless.

37
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
So now we're going to download our data.

38
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
And we prepared that ahead of time so that you don't have to watch us download data.

39
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.000
It's sort of like a cooking show in that sense.

40
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.000
And then the output file will be called lesson2wiki.csv.zip.

41
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.000
And it's a file sitting on Dropbox somewhere with a long, long URL.

42
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
And then after it gets downloaded, we unzip that file.

43
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.000
And you'll have a CSV file locally in your notebook.

44
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
So once we do that, then we're going to create a data frame out of it.

45
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
And so this is a data frame using DFS shorthand for data frame.

46
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.000
And we're using Pandas to read the CSV file.

47
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
So that's reads underscore CSV, and we're reading the file that we just unzipped.

48
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
And then let's just take a quick look at the data that we have.

49
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Okay, so we used DataFrame.head to look at the data.

50
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
And so there's a few columns of interest.

51
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
One is the unique identifier for the data.

52
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.000
There's the metadata that has the source of the articles and the content.

53
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.000
And then there's the vector embeddings themselves under values.

54
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.000
And these are good examples of what a vector embedding looks like.

55
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.000
It's really just a list of floating point numbers.

56
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.000
And now we'll prepare the embeddings and upload them into Pinecone.

57
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.000
So we'll create an array called prepped.

58
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.000
That's going to save what we store into Pinecone.

59
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.000
And then we will iterate through the data frame.

60
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.000
So we'll iterate through the data frame.

61
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
We'll use QDM again to get a progress bar.

62
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.000
And then we will get the metadata from it.

63
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
Because that's a string, we're going to use literal eval from the AST package

64
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.000
to get a dictionary back.

65
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.000
And then we're going to append it to what we just created.

66
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
And this is how you create a vector embedding in Pinecone,

67
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.000
is this long dictionary of values that I cut and pasted here

68
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
because it's a long value expended to.

69
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
So what we have here is really a tuple again.

70
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.000
You have an ID that's a unique identifier for the embedding.

71
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:36.000
You have the values, which is really the vector embedding itself.

72
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.000
And then you have metadata.

73
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
And we just took the metadata we had from the line above.

74
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.000
And this is the article information.

75
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Now what we're going to do is upload that data into Pinecone.

76
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.000
So what we want to do is do it in batches.

77
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
So if the length of that prepared array is greater than 200,

78
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
we're going to do it in batches of 200.

79
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
And then we're going to insert it into Pinecone.

80
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
And then once it's inserted, we're going to clear that out

81
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
so that we can do it all over again.

82
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.000
So, again, a Pinecone embedding is really three things.

83
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.000
There's the ID that's a unique identifier for it.

84
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.000
There's the values that's the vector embedding.

85
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
And then there's metadata that goes with the vector embedding.

86
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.000
In this case, it's the article metadata.

87
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.000
And now what we're going to do is upload data

88
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.000
in batches of 200 vectors at a time into Pinecone.

89
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.000
So we'll check to see if we've stored 200 elements into this prepped array.

90
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.000
And if so, then we upsort the data, and then we clear it out again.

91
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.000
And so we'll run that.

92
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.000
And so we'll see tqdm do its thing while we upload data into Pinecone.

93
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
So we finished uploading our data into Pinecone,

94
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.000
and let's verify what was uploaded.

95
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So we will use index.describeIndexStats and look at it.

96
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.000
All right, we have 10,000 elements, just as we had hoped.

97
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Now we're going to use OpenAI.

98
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.000
So what we're going to do is get our OpenAI API key.

99
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.000
And that is utilizing this package that we created that we keep mentioning

100
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.000
called dlai-utils, and we're going to get our API key.

101
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.000
And we're going to get a pointer to the OpenAI client.

102
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
And then I have a small helper routine that I've defined

103
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.000
to help you get back a vector embedding from OpenAI.

104
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
So this is called getEmbeddings, and what this does is

105
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
it's basically taking an array of text,

106
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.000
and it's going to use the Ada embedding model

107
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.000
and return to you vector embeddings for the text in the array of articles.

108
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.000
And it just basically just returns it sort of blindly.

109
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
By the way, you'll notice that we had that batch of 200

110
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.000
that I talked about earlier before.

111
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.000
Feel free to try and play with that number and see what you can get.

112
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.000
I found that, for me personally, it worked great at 200.

113
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Pinecone recommends that you do something between 100 and 500,

114
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.000
but I personally found that 200 was the fastest for me.

115
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.000
I'd love to hear from all of you out there

116
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.000
taking the lesson what was fastest for you.

117
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.000
And now we're all set up.

118
00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.000
We have our data sitting in Pinecone.

119
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
We've connected to OpenAI, or we've prepared OpenAI,

120
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
and we're ready to execute some queries.

121
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.000
So let's talk about the Berlin Wall for just a moment,

122
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:35.000
and we will ask, what is the Berlin Wall?

123
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.000
And then we will build an embedding out of that query.

124
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.000
And then we will run a query against Pinecone.

125
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.000
And this is how we query Pinecone again.

126
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.000
It takes a pointer to the embedding that we just got above.

127
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.000
We're going to return the top three results.

128
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.000
Again, feel free to play with that.

129
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.000
You can change it to 5 or 10 or whatever you want to look at.

130
00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.000
In this case, I only want to see 3 for demo purposes.

131
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.000
And then we want the article data itself,

132
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.000
and that's going to be sitting in the metadata section,

133
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.000
and we want to have that setting set to true

134
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.000
so that we get the metadata back.

135
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.000
And then what we'll do is parse the response out.

136
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.000
So we get back an object from Pinecone,

137
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.000
and we're going to extract out from the matches object

138
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.000
the text inside the metadata.

139
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.000
So that's what this comprehension does.

140
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.000
And then what we're going to do is look at it

141
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.000
by joining it to the newline character.

142
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:32.000
Now we're done with the setup,

143
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.000
and we're going to query Pinecone and see the results come back.

144
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:36.000
And so here we go.

145
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.000
We've got three articles.

146
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.000
We asked for three because we set top K to three

147
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.000
that have information about the Berlin Wall.

148
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.000
So this is a lot of text, and it's disconnected

149
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.000
because we have three discrete articles.

150
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.000
But it would be really nice to get that summarized

151
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.000
in something that's readable for folks

152
00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.000
in case we want to use it for our homework assignment

153
00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:56.000
or preparing a memo for work.

154
00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.000
That's definitely what I would do,

155
00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.000
and that's what we're going to do next.

156
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.000
And so we're going to prepare

157
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.000
through what's called prompt engineering.

158
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.000
We're going to build a small prompt

159
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.000
out of those articles that we just saw in Pinecone,

160
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.000
and it's going to take the following format.

161
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.000
We're going to say, write an article titled,

162
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.000
colon, what is the Berlin Wall?

163
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.000
We're basically instructing OpenAI to write an article for us

164
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.000
based on the Berlin Wall.

165
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.000
And what we're going to do is, again,

166
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.000
get our embeddings using the get embeddings helper function

167
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.000
that we just asked for.

168
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.000
We'll run this all over again.

169
00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
We just did this, but that's okay.

170
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:30.000
It's fast.

171
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.000
We're going to run the query.

172
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.000
And now we're actually going to build our prompt,

173
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.000
and this is going to be fairly sophisticated

174
00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.000
compared to just asking it a simple question.

175
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.000
So what we're going to do is iterate through the matches object

176
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.000
that we just got back from Pinecone

177
00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
and get back that text,

178
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.000
and that's what we're going to store in the contexts array.

179
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.000
And then we're going to have a prompt start and a prompt end.

180
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.000
And prompt start is going to say,

181
00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.000
answer the question based on the context below.

182
00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:01.000
Note that it has a slash end, slash end at the end.

183
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.000
That's fairly important.

184
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And then context.

185
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.000
And what's going to be inside the context

186
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:09.000
is those three articles that we just got back.

187
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
But before we do that, we're going to define the end,

188
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:17.000
and then what we'll do is join the start to our context to our end.

189
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.000
And our end is basically the question, right,

190
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.000
that we have above here,

191
00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:26.000
and then we want to ask OpenAI to give us back an answer.

192
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.000
And so what we're going to do is define a tuple called prompt,

193
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:39.000
and prompt is going to have prompt start

194
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.000
plus some key newline characters and dashes.

195
00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.000
That's just helping OpenAI recognize

196
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:48.000
that we're doing some prompt engineering here.

197
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:51.000
And then we have prompt end that we just declared above.

198
00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.000
So we have prompt start,

199
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.000
answer the question based on the context below.

200
00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:59.000
We have context, which is the result we got back from Pinecone,

201
00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:01.000
and then we have the end, which is question colon,

202
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:03.000
and it was the question we're asking.

203
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.000
In this case, it's what's the Berlin Wall,

204
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.000
and then we're asking it for an answer,

205
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.000
and that will be stored in the tuple named prompt.

206
00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.000
And let's take a look at what we have.

207
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.000
All right.

208
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.000
Answer the question based on the context below,

209
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.000
the context, which is the summary of each of our responses from Pinecone,

210
00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.000
and then write an article titled what is the Berlin Wall answer.

211
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.000
All right.

212
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.000
Now we're ready to send that to OpenAI.

213
00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:34.000
And so what we're going to do is talk to OpenAI in a very typical fashion.

214
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.000
We're going to use the completions API.

215
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.000
We're going to use GPT 3.5 turbo, and we're going to ask for 1,500 tokens max.

216
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.000
And, again, all of this is things that you can tune and play with yourself.

217
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.000
Feel free to change max tokens or temperature.

218
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.000
And then we're going to do one of my favorite things,

219
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:57.000
which is this little toy function to print out a delimiter to keep everything clean,

220
00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:02.000
and then we're going to print out a response from OpenAI.

221
00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.000
All right.

222
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.000
And there we have it.

223
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
We have a nicely written article about the Berlin Wall, right?

224
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:12.000
The Berlin Wall, known as the Iron Curtain, was a physical and ideological barrier.

225
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.000
Okay, that's not so great, but the article is well written,

226
00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.000
and it's using the information that we got back from Pinecone.

227
00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.000
So in this lesson, we looked at data that came back from Pinecone.

228
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.000
We got three discrete documents back from it.

229
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.000
We took that, built a prompt-engineered query into OpenAI,

230
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.000
and got back a nicely summarized response from OpenAI that's well written

231
00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.000
and probably usable for your homework assignment even.

232
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.000
In the next lesson, we're going to build a basic recommendation system.

